"GPT-5.1-Codex-Max System Card | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-1-codex-max-system-card/)\nciteturn2527view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-1-codex-max-system-card/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 126\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: November 19, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Publication cite4†Safety L16: \nL17: # GPT‑5.1‑Codex‑Max System Card\nL18: \nL19: cite5†Read the System Card(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L20: \nL21: Share\nL22: ## Introduction\nL23: GPT‑5.1‑Codex‑Max is our new frontier agentic coding model. It is built on an update to our foundational reasoning model trained on agentic tasks across software engineering, math, research, medicine, computer use and more. It is our first model natively trained to operate across multiple context windows through a process called compaction, coherently working over millions of tokens in a single task.\nL24: Like its predecessors, GPT‑5.1‑Codex‑Max was trained on real-world software engineering tasks like PR creation, code review, frontend coding and Q&A.\nL25: This system card outlines the comprehensive safety measures implemented for GPT‑5.1-Codex-Max. It details both model-level mitigations, such as specialized safety training for harmful tasks and prompt injections, and product-level mitigations like agent sandboxing and configurable network access.\nL26: GPT‑5.1‑Codex‑Max was evaluated under our Preparedness Framework. It is very capable in the cybersecurity domain but does not reach High capability on cybersecurity. We expect current trends of rapidly increasing capability to continue, and for models to cross the High cybersecurity threshold in the near future. Like other recent models, it is being treated as High capability on biology, and is being deployed with the corresponding suite of safeguards we use for GPT‑5.\nL27: It does not reach High capability on AI self-improvement.\nL28:   * cite6†2025 L29:   * cite7†System Cards L30: ## Author\nL31: \nL32: OpenAI\nL33: ## Keep reading\nL34: \nL35: cite8†View all L36: \nL37: cite9†Towards safety cases for frontier AI training SafetySep 28, 2026 L38: \nL39: cite10†How we will do better for Australia CompanySep 28, 2026 L40: \nL41: cite11†Sam Altman’s remarks at the United Nations Security Council Global AffairsSep 23, 2026 L42: \nL43: Research\nL44: \nL45:   * cite12†Research Index L46:   * cite13†Research Overview L47:   * cite14†Economic Research L48: \nL49: Latest Advancements\nL50: \nL51:   * cite15†GPT-6.1 Sol L52:   * cite16†GPT-6 Astra L53:   * cite17†GPT-5.6 L54:   * cite18†GPT-5.5 L55: \nL56: Safety\nL57:   * cite19†Safety Approach L58:   * cite20†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L59:   * cite21†Security & Privacy L60:   * cite22†Trust & Transparency L61: \nL62: Products\nL63:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L64:   * cite23†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L65:   * cite24†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L66:   * cite25†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L67:   * cite26†Codex†chatgpt.com L68:   * cite27†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L69:   * cite28†Release Notes L70: \nL71: API Platform\nL72: \nL73:   * cite29†Overview L74:   * cite30†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L75:   * cite31†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L76: \nL77: Business\nL78:   * cite32†Overview L79:   * cite33†Solutions L80:   * cite34†Resources L